.TH CVEC_FIND 3 "2026-09-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_find \- find the first element with a given value (type-inferred)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

size_t idx = cvec_find(v, elem);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro is a type-inferred wrapper for cvector_find() that accepts a
value, an rvalue or a literal included. The macro converts the needle to
the declared element type of the vector and compares the elements with the
default comparison procedure of that type, so equality is the equality of
the type and not the equality of its object representation. chashmap and
cbstmap use the same idea of equality for a key of the same type: for
example, -0.0 and 0.0 are one value, and a long double compares by value
rather than through the padding bytes that its representation carries on
some targets.

A char*, signed char* or unsigned char* element (uint8_t* is an
unsigned char*) is a NUL-terminated string to this library, and the
comparison reads it with strcmp(). Do not search a vector of pointers to
binary buffers this way: a buffer with no NUL byte is read past its end.
Search such a vector with cvector_find(3) and a comparator that compares
the pointers, or declare the element type as void*, whose byte-wise
equality is the identity of the pointer.

An element type that is an array, such as char[64], takes an array or a
string literal as elem. The macro zero-fills its copy to the element size
and copies the bytes of elem into it, up to the smaller of the two sizes,
exactly as cvec_push(3) stores such a value. A pointer as elem for an
array element type is a compile error, because the macro cannot see the
size of what it points at.

A char* element can be NULL, and so can the needle. The default string
comparison procedure puts NULL before every string that is not NULL, and
NULL is equal only to another NULL, so a search for NULL in a sparse char*
vector gives the index of the first empty slot. The macro dereferences
nothing.

Some element types have no default comparison procedure: a bool, a
struct, a union, a pointer to anything other than char, and a fixed-size
char array. For such a type, the comparison falls back to byte-wise
equality over the whole representation of the element, including the
padding bytes of a struct. With GCC 11 or later, the macro sets the
padding bytes of its own copy of elem to zero, and cvec_push(3) does the
same for each element that it stores, so the macro finds a struct that
went in through cvec_push(3) with any value whose members are equal; the
padding bytes of that value have no effect. An element that went in
through cvector_push_back(3), cvector_append_array(3) or an assignment
through cvec_at(3) keeps the padding bytes that the caller wrote. With a
compiler that offers no __builtin_clear_padding, such as Clang, the
padding bytes of the macro copies are unspecified; search for a padded
struct with cvector_find(3) and a comparator that reads the members.
cvec_find compiles for every element type, while cvec_sort is
deliberately different: an ordering cannot come from the bytes of an
element in the way that equality can, so cvec_sort has no fallback and
rejects such an element type at compile time.

Use cvector_find() directly when you need a custom comparator, or when
you want byte-wise equality for a type that has a default comparison
procedure.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v     Vector to search
elem  Element value to search for. It can be an rvalue or an lvalue.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Zero-based index of the first element that matches.
ccol_invalid_size when there is no match.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
O(n) complexity
The macro compares with the default comparison procedure of the element
type.
The macro orders a NULL char* element, and a NULL needle for a char*
vector, without dereferencing either of them.
For a type that has no default comparison procedure, the macro falls back
to byte-wise equality, so a struct with padding bytes compares correctly
only when both copies carry zero padding. See DESCRIPTION.
The macro compiles for every element type; cvec_sort does not.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cvec_construct(vec, int);
cvec_push(vec, 10);
cvec_push(vec, 20);
cvec_push(vec, 30);
size_t idx = cvec_find(vec, 20);   // idx == 1
size_t nf  = cvec_find(vec, 99);   // nf  == ccol_invalid_size
cvec_destroy(vec);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvector_find (3),
.BR cvec_push (3),
.BR cvec_sort (3)
